{"code":"NEUG","name":"NEUG","term":"FA26","termName":"Fall 2026","courses":{"NEUG 221":{"t":"Advanced Topics in Neurosciences","u":"1","desc":"Specialized advanced topic areas in neurosciences will be addressed in an interactive seminar course format. A different specific topic will be considered each quarter as announced in advance. Students will each present an aspect of the topic area and participate in discussions.","pre":"priority given to neuroscience PhD students, or consent of instructor. (F,W,S)","unl":[],"hist":[],"ge":[],"gpa":null,"aRate":null,"terms":0,"offered":1,"seatUrl":"https://tss.ucsd.edu/fiori#ZUSModule-display?TileType=MYMOD&/Detail/EventPackage/SM/10401/00000000/0/0/0/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/154902/2026/2/?","tss":"https://tss.ucsd.edu/fiori#ZUSModule-display?TileType=MYMOD&/Detail/EventPackage/SM/10401/00000000/0/0/0/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/154902/2026/2/?","res":"Limited to academic level: Graduate","fa":["Binhai Zheng"],"seatsAt":"2026-10-23T16:23Z","profs":[{"i":"Binhai Zheng","g":0,"A":0,"B":0,"C":0,"D":0,"F":0,"W":0,"P":0,"NP":0,"n":0,"y":null,"cur":1,"rq":null,"rd":null,"rw":null,"rn":null,"rid":null}],"sec":[["A00","LE","Tu","11:00a","12:50p","Biomedical Research Facility II","BRF2 5A03","Binhai Zheng",6,12,0,0,6,"E 00009849",["154902"],660,770,"BRF2","AC","","2026-10-23T16:23Z"]]},"NEUG 231":{"t":"Tools for Experimental Data Analysis","u":"4","desc":"This course will cover a set of general data analysis methods that are broadly applicable in many different subdisciplines of psychology/neuroscience. Topics include model fitting, information theory, Fourier analysis, and machine learning. NEUG 231 is renumbered from NEU 231. Students may only receive credit for one of the following: PSYC 231, NEUG 231, NEU 231. Recommended preparation: MATLAB, Python, C, Java, R, or any related language.","pre":null,"unl":[],"hist":[],"ge":[],"gpa":null,"aRate":null,"terms":0,"offered":0,"seatUrl":null,"tss":null,"res":null,"fa":[],"profs":[],"sec":[]},"NEUG 240":{"t":"Mathematical Foundations for Computational Neuroscience","u":"3","desc":"This course teaches mathematical concepts in neuroscience to students inexperienced in computation and mathematics. Topics from linear algebra, calculus, information theory, digital signal processing, graph theory, and machine learning will be covered. An optional weekly tutorial will teach computing basics. Priority enrollment is given to PhD students. MS students may enroll with instructor approval. Calculus AB is recommended but not required.","pre":null,"unl":[],"hist":[],"ge":[],"gpa":null,"aRate":null,"terms":0,"offered":0,"seatUrl":null,"tss":null,"res":null,"fa":[],"profs":[],"sec":[]},"NEUG 257":{"t":"Neuroanatomy","u":"4","desc":"Neuroanatomy with functional correlates, concentrating on mammals but also including other chordates and other phyla. Includes laboratory sessions with whole brains, gross sections, and histology. Labs are often simultaneous with lectures to permit interactive identification and contextualization of anatomical structures.","pre":"for neurosciences graduate students in major codes NE75 and NE78. Other disciplines may enroll with consent of instructor of record. (W)","unl":[],"hist":[],"ge":[],"gpa":null,"aRate":null,"terms":0,"offered":0,"seatUrl":null,"tss":null,"res":null,"fa":[],"profs":[],"sec":[]},"NEUG 265":{"t":"Mechanisms of Pain","u":"2","desc":"Anatomical and pharmacological substrates activated following tissue or nerve injury will be discussed. Changes in afferents and spinal cord, including increased expressions of channels and receptors during chronic pain states will be emphasized. Clinical relevance and treatment will be included. (S/U grades only.) (F)","pre":null,"unl":[],"hist":[],"ge":[],"gpa":null,"aRate":null,"terms":0,"offered":1,"seatUrl":"https://tss.ucsd.edu/fiori#ZUSModule-display?TileType=MYMOD&/Detail/EventPackage/SM/10732/00000000/0/0/0/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/155000/2026/2/